==============================================================================
PART 1 -- the fields, verified against their defining relations
==============================================================================
  F_2: q = 2, generator z = 1 (code), z^2 = 1 equals 1: True
  F_9: q = 9, generator z = 3 (code), z^2 = 4 equals 4: True
  F_25: q = 25, generator z = 5 (code), z^2 = 8 equals 8: True
  F_9  units and orders: 1:1, 2:2, 3:8, 4:4, 5:8, 6:8, 7:8, 8:4
  F_25 units of order dividing 3: [1, 11, 18]

==============================================================================
PART 2 -- Example 1 (p.5): A_1 = F_2[x1,x2]/<t1,t2>
==============================================================================
  t1 = x^5 + x^4 + 1, irreducible factor degrees: [2, 3]
  t2 = x^4 + x^2 + x, irreducible factor degrees: [1, 3]
  (paper p.5 writes t2 = x2(x2^2+x2+1); that product is x^3+x^2+x.  The degrees that match the paper's own count of six are [1, 3].)
  #components  N = sum gcd(d_i,e_j) = 6   (paper p.5: six)
  dim_2 A      = sum d_i*e_j = 20 = deg t1 * deg t2 = 5*4
  class sizes |S(nu)| = [2, 3, 3, 3, 3, 6]; sum = 20
  |A| = 2^20 = 1048576   vs   |F_2^N| = 2^6 = 64
  => A -> F_2^N injective would need 1048576 <= 64: False
  => Example 1 is NOT the split case: class sizes are not all 1.

==============================================================================
PART 3 -- Example 2 (p.18-20): A = F_9[x1,x2]/<t1,t2>
==============================================================================
  roots of z^2 + 2z + 2 in F_9: [3, 7]  (the naming of omega)
   omega = 3: t1 = x^2+x+w irreducible: True,  t2 = x^2+w^5 x+2 irreducible: True
   omega = 7: t1 = x^2+x+w irreducible: True,  t2 = x^2+w^5 x+2 irreducible: True
  using omega = 7 (the two choices give the same verdicts)
  dim_F9 A = 2*2 = 4;   |A| = 9^4 = 6561
  idempotents of A: 4;  primitive: 2  =>  N = 2
  dim_F9 of each component e_S A: [2, 2]  (predicted |S(nu)| = 2 each)
  sum of component degrees = 4 = dim_F9 A = 4
  => A ~= F_81 x F_81, not F_9 x F_9
  |F_9^N| = 9^2 = 81   vs   |A| = 6561
  => psi: A -> F_9^2 cannot be injective (pigeonhole), so not the 'bijection' of p.17

  x^7 - 1: irreducible factor degrees [1, 3, 3], multiplicities all 1: True
  x^7 - w^2: irreducible factor degrees [1, 3, 3], multiplicities all 1: True
  g1 = x^3 + w^3 x^2 + w^5 x + 2 divides x^7-1 : True
  g2 = x + w^2 divides x^7 - w^2                : True
  deg g1 = 3, deg g2 = 1

  A-module C = <g>: rank over F_9 of {b*x^i*g} = 20
    (= sum_S |S| (n - deg g_S) = 2*4 + 2*6 = 20)
  paper prints dim = sum_S (n - deg g_S) = 10
  claimed Gray-image length nN = 7*2 = 14
  dim_F9 C = 20 > nN = 14 ? True
  => no injective F_9-linear map C -> F_9^14 exists;
     the claimed [14, 10, 4]_9 is impossible.

==============================================================================
PART 4 -- Example 3 (p.20-21): A = F_25[x1,x2]/<t1,t2>
==============================================================================
  roots of z^2 + 4z + 2 in F_25: [5, 21]
   omega = 5: t1 = x^2+x+w^13 irreducible: True;  t2 = x^2+w^3 x+1 irreducible: False
   omega = 21: t1 = x^2+x+w^13 irreducible: True;  t2 = x^2+w^3 x+1 irreducible: False
  using omega = 21
  t1 factor degrees: [2];  t2 factor degrees: [1, 1]
  => N = sum gcd = 2;  dim_F25 A = 4;  class sizes = [2, 2]
  |A| = 25^4 = 390625   vs   |F_25^N| = 25^2 = 625

  x^13 - 1: irreducible factor degrees [1, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2]
  x^13 - w^8: irreducible factor degrees [1, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2]
  g1 = (x+4)(x^2+w x+1) = [4, 5, 20, 1], deg 3; divides x^13-1: True
  g2 = x + w^20, deg 1; divides x^13-w^8: True

  primitive idempotents computed by brute force over 390625 elements...
  primitive idempotents: 2  (matches N = 2)
  dim_F25 of each component e_S A: [2, 2]  (predicted |S(nu)| = 2 each)
  sum of component degrees = 4 = dim_F25 A = 4
  => A ~= F_625 x F_625, not F_25 x F_25
  A-module C = <g>: rank over F_25 = 44
    (= sum_S |S| (n - deg g_S) = 2*10 + 2*12 = 44)
  paper prints 22;  claimed length nN = 13*2 = 26
  dim_F25 C = 44 > 26 ? True

==============================================================================
PART 5 -- the Gray map of p.17: what it can and cannot be
==============================================================================
  eq.(10):  psi : A -> F_q^N,  a |-> (a_{S(nu_1)},...,a_{S(nu_N)}) M,
            with M in GL_N(F_q) and N = |S-hat|.
  p.17:     'the map psi is bijective linear isometry from A^n to F_q^{nN}'

  A bijection A -> F_q^N requires |A| = q^N, i.e. dim_q A = N.
  ring      dim_q A   N     |A|         |F_q^N|     injective?
  Ex 1      20        6     1048576     64          NO
  Ex 2      4         2     6561        81          NO
  Ex 3      4         2     390625      625         NO

  Even the *codomain length* is forced: psi(C) is a code of length nN,
  so dim_q psi(C) <= nN.  But psi is claimed linear and bijective, so
  dim_q psi(C) = dim_q C.  The two computed dim_q C (20, 44) exceed the
  claimed nN (14, 26) for BOTH examples, independently of distances.

  What the paper's own arithmetic computes: sum_S dim_{A_S}, i.e. the
  dimension with the factor |S(nu)| suppressed.  Reproduced exactly by
  code/final_check_B.py (component-level table).

==============================================================================
PART 6 -- independent verification of the reported F_9 isometry witness
==============================================================================
  field: F_9 = F_3[z]/(z^2+2z+2); codes 0..8 mean a0 + a1*z.
  z = 3, z^2 = 4, z^4 = 2 (= 2, the norm, lying in F_3)
  roots of z^2+2z+2 (candidate names for omega): [3, 7]
  --- naming omega = 3 (A) ---
    source ring R_a = F_9[x]/(x^5 - 4) with a = omega^2 = 4
    target ring R_b = F_9[x]/(x^5 - 3) with b = omega   = 3
    check a = b^2 : True
    g = 2 + 6x + x^2 ;  g | x^5 - a : True
    dim C = 3, |C| = 729;  dim image = 3, |image| = 729
    C is 4-constacyclic : True
    image is 3-constacyclic: True
    |hull_1(C)| = 81  -> dim = 2
    |hull_1(image)| = 1 -> dim = 0
    weight distributions equal: True
    monomial scalings a_i = [1, 1, 1, 3, 3];  norms a_i^4 = [1, 1, 1, 2, 2] (constant? False)
    ==> HULL DIMENSION CHANGES: 2 -> 0

  --- naming omega = 7 (B) ---
    source ring R_a = F_9[x]/(x^5 - 8) with a = omega^2 = 8
    target ring R_b = F_9[x]/(x^5 - 7) with b = omega   = 7
    check a = b^2 : True
    g = 2 + 6x + x^2 ;  g | x^5 - a : False
    dim C = 3, |C| = 729;  dim image = 3, |image| = 729
    C is 8-constacyclic : False
    image is 7-constacyclic: False
    |hull_1(C)| = 81  -> dim = 2
    |hull_1(image)| = 1 -> dim = 0
    weight distributions equal: True
    monomial scalings a_i = [1, 1, 1, 7, 7];  norms a_i^4 = [1, 1, 1, 2, 2] (constant? False)
    ==> HULL DIMENSION CHANGES: 2 -> 0

  Independence note: the script does not read the earlier outputs; it
  rebuilds the field, the rings, both codes, the Hermitian dual and the
  hull from scratch, and it tests BOTH conjugate namings of omega.

==============================================================================
PART 7 -- the q = 4 ring of Table 1, and the table's lengths
==============================================================================
  F_4 = F_2[z]/(z^2+z+1), z = 2 (primitive: order 3)
  t1 = x^2 + x + w  irreducible over F_4: True
  t2 = x^2 + w x + w irreducible over F_4: True
  => dim_4 A = 2*2 = 4 and N = gcd(2,2) = 2 for the q = 4 ring too

  Table 1 (p.22) as printed: n and the length of psi(C)
    n = 5   length = 10    2n = 10   ok
    n = 4   length = 8     2n = 8    ok
    n = 5   length = 10    2n = 10   ok
    n = 5   length = 10    2n = 10   ok
    n = 5   length = 10    2n = 10   ok
    n = 6   length = 12    2n = 12   ok
    n = 7   length = 14    2n = 14   ok
    n = 7   length = 14    2n = 14   ok
    n = 7   length = 14    2n = 14   ok
    n = 13  length = 26    2n = 26   ok
    n = 16  length = 32    2n = 32   ok
    n = 20  length = 40    2n = 40   ok
    n = 26  length = 52    2n = 52   ok
  all 13 rows have length exactly 2n: True
  Since dim_q A = 4 for every ring used in the table, A^n has F_q-dimension
  4n; a bijective linear Gray map would therefore have to land in F_q^{4n}.
  Every printed length is 2n, i.e. a factor 2 short -- the same factor
  |S(nu)| = 2 that is missing from the dimensions.

